# Mintlify technical writing

You write Database Fundamentals docs in Mintlify MDX. Follow `AGENTS.md`.

Always start new lessons from `/templates/` (how-to, tutorial, explanation, reference). Read the matching template before you write. The Templates group is `hidden: true` in `docs.json` so learners do not see it. Do not add it back to the visible sidebar. Do not link `/templates/` from lesson pages.

Read `docs.json` and similar pages before you add or rewrite content. Prefer searching current Mintlify docs over training data. Use the [docs.json schema](https://mintlify.com/docs.json) for site config and navigation.

## Language and style

- Second person ("you") for instructions
- Active voice; present tense for current state, future tense for outcomes
- Sentence case for headings
- One idea per sentence; parallel lists, headings, and steps
- Bold UI labels; backtick paths, commands, SQL, and identifiers
- Define jargon on first use
- No marketing language, filler, or "simply / just / easily"
- Do not put author process or outline notes on the page ("group it here", "we put these together so", "this section covers"). Explain the idea; let the heading and layout show the structure.
- Say **table**, **row**, **column**, **primary key**, **query**; say **database** only for the whole system; prefer **schema** over **model** unless you mean an ORM
- Do not invent dialects, vendors, or features that are not on the page
- Do not document Mintlify, Cursor, or this repo on lesson pages

## Organization

- Lead with the outcome (inverted pyramid)
- Basic concepts before advanced ones
- Prerequisites before procedures
- Expected result after each major step
- Keyword-rich, descriptive headings
- Troubleshooting for likely failures
- Verification when success is not obvious

When turning a lecture transcript into pages: outline from the idea sequence, not from example-named YouTube chapters. Q&A usually closes a section. Keep example datasets in code; keep titles and nav general.

## Page structure

Every page starts with YAML frontmatter:

```yaml
---
title: "Clear, specific, keyword-rich title"
description: "What the thing is, or what it does — not a syllabus for the page"
---
```

- File names are kebab-case: `primary-keys.mdx`
- Explanation titles are the concept name (`Tables`, `SQL`), not `About …`. Do not nest a page inside a group of the same name. SQL family ideas such as aggregator functions and scalar functions live on the SQL page. Keyword lookup stays in Reference.
- Reference titles are the clause, keyword, or function (`SELECT`, `FROM`, `COUNT`), not `… reference`. Nest keywords under Read, Filter, Sort, Summarize, Relate, Combine, Write, Define, and Constrain. Operators such as `=` stay on WHERE.
- Explanation `description` states what the concept is. Reference `description` states what the construct does. How-to and tutorial `description` state the task.
- Mintlify shows `description` under the title. Do not restate it in the first body paragraph. Start the body with a new fact. Do not open with "This page explains", "This guide shows you", or "In this tutorial you" if that repeats the title or description.
- Internal links are root-relative with no extension: `/quickstart`
- Add every new lesson to a visible group in `docs.json` navigation. Keep `/templates/` in the hidden Templates group only. Topic groups (for example Querying) hold how-tos in working order (open, select, filter, match, sort, summarize), then the tutorial last. Relating holds list, nest, join, combine, group, then the tutorial. Designing holds show schema, create, constrain, alter, apply a schema file, then the tutorial. Writing holds insert, import CSV, delete, delete related, update, trigger, soft-delete, then the tutorial. Viewing holds create a view, create a temporary view, use a CTE, partition, hide columns, write through a view, then the tutorial. Optimizing holds time a query, create an index, explain a query plan, vacuum, run a transaction, then the tutorial. Scaling nests MySQL-only how-tos under MySQL (connect, create table, describe, modify column, stored procedure) and PostgreSQL under PostgreSQL (connect); grant privileges, prepared statement, and the tutorial stay at the Scaling root. Do not add Tutorial or How-to as group names. **Concepts** nests explanations under Foundations, Structure, Design, Change, Speed, and Scale. **Reference** nests keywords under Read, Filter, Sort, Summarize, Relate, Combine, Write, Optimize, Define, Access, and Constrain. Do not nest Concepts as SQL under SQL.
- Body headings start at H2 (the title is H1)
- Images live in `/images`, use `<Frame>` plus descriptive alt text
- Actionable link text; never "click here"
- Code blocks always have a language tag; use realistic names (`orders`, `customer_id`), not `foo`/`bar`
- Never put real secrets in examples
- Mark unknowns with `{/* TODO: ... */}` instead of guessing
- After edits: `mint validate` and `mint broken-links`

## Component selection

- **Steps** for sequential procedures
- **Tabs** for alternative approaches or dialects
- **CodeGroup** for the same example in more than one SQL dialect or language
- **Accordions** for optional or troubleshooting detail
- **Note** extra context; **Tip** advice; **Info** neutral background; **Warning** destructive risk; **Check** success
- **Card** / **CardGroup** for linked next steps
- **ParamField** / **ResponseField** / **Expandable** for reference of clauses, arguments, and nested results
- **RequestExample** / **ResponseExample** only if you document an HTTP API
- Do not add components that only decorate the page

## Callouts

```mdx
<Note>Supporting context the reader can skip.</Note>
<Tip>A shortcut or better default.</Tip>
<Warning>Data loss, irreversible `DROP`, or breaking change.</Warning>
<Info>Neutral background, such as which SQL this example uses.</Info>
<Check>How to confirm the change worked.</Check>
```

## Code

```sql orders.sql
SELECT customer_id, COUNT(*) AS order_count
FROM orders
GROUP BY customer_id;
```

````mdx
<CodeGroup>
```sql PostgreSQL
SELECT customer_id
FROM orders
WHERE created_at >= DATE '2024-01-01';
```

```sql SQLite
SELECT customer_id
FROM orders
WHERE created_at >= '2024-01-01';
```
</CodeGroup>
````

## Structure

````mdx
<Steps>
<Step title="Create the table">
  Run the `CREATE TABLE` statement.

  <Check>Confirm the table exists in the schema listing.</Check>
</Step>
<Step title="Add the primary key">
  ```sql
  ALTER TABLE orders
  ADD CONSTRAINT orders_pk PRIMARY KEY (order_id);
  ```

  <Warning>Do not run this until duplicate `order_id` values are gone.</Warning>
</Step>
</Steps>
````

````mdx
<Tabs>
<Tab title="PostgreSQL">
  ```sql
  CREATE INDEX orders_customer_id_idx ON orders (customer_id);
  ```
</Tab>
<Tab title="SQLite">
  ```sql
  CREATE INDEX orders_customer_id_idx ON orders (customer_id);
  ```
</Tab>
</Tabs>
````

```mdx
<AccordionGroup>
<Accordion title="Query returns no rows">
  - Confirm the table has data
  - Check the `WHERE` clause types (text vs number)
</Accordion>
<Accordion title="Primary key error">
  Duplicate values in the key column block the constraint
</Accordion>
</AccordionGroup>
```

```mdx
<CardGroup cols={2}>
<Card title="Tables" icon="table" href="/concepts/tables">
  What a table is and when to use one.
</Card>
<Card title="Filter rows" icon="list-check" href="/how-to/filter-rows">
  Limit query results with a `WHERE` clause.
</Card>
</CardGroup>
```

## Reference fields

```mdx
<ParamField body="customer_id" type="integer" required>
  Identifies the customer. Must match `customers.customer_id`.
</ParamField>

<ResponseField name="order_count" type="integer" required>
  Number of rows in `orders` for that customer.
</ResponseField>
```

